        <div id="what-it-runs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What 100 to 300 Watts of AC Actually Runs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A battery pack with an AC outlet is a small lithium battery, a charge controller and an inverter in one box. The inverter is what turns the battery's low-voltage DC into the 120-volt AC that comes out of a wall socket, and its continuous rating is the hard ceiling on what you can plug in. In this size class that ceiling is usually 300 watts, with a few models reaching 600. Everything in this guide is about living inside that number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three hundred watts covers more than people expect. A laptop charger draws somewhere between 45 and 100 watts while charging and less once the battery is full. A CPAP without its heated humidifier typically pulls 30 to 60 watts. A cable modem and Wi-Fi router together often sit around 15 to 30 watts. A 40-inch LED TV, a box fan, a string of LED lights, a camera battery charger, a drone charger and most cordless tool battery chargers all land comfortably under 150 watts. Run two or three of those at once and you are still inside a 300-watt inverter.
          </p>

          <p>
            What it will not run is anything that makes heat with a resistive element. A hair dryer, a coffee maker, a toaster, a kettle, a space heater, an electric blanket on high, a curling iron and a microwave all draw somewhere between 600 and 1,500 watts. Plug one into a 300-watt pack and the inverter shuts off within a second, which is the protection doing its job. If heating or cooking is the point, you need a much larger station from our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station guide</a>, and even then the battery empties quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The awkward middle is motors and compressors. A mini fridge or small 12-volt-style cooler might run at 50 to 100 watts but draw several times that for a fraction of a second when the compressor starts. A small aquarium pump is easy; a sump pump is not. A blender, a shop vac and a circular saw are well beyond this class. Read the wattage on the appliance's label, and when the label lists amps, multiply by 120 to get a rough watt figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is a different job from charging phones. If all you need is to top up phones and tablets during a blackout, a USB power bank is lighter and cheaper, and our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">best portable chargers for power outages</a> covers that. You buy a battery pack with an outlet because something you own only takes a plug: a CPAP, a laptop brick, a fan, a TV, a router, a medical or hobby device that was never designed to run on USB.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Comparison Table
          </h2>

          <p>
            The six packs below are all lithium iron phosphate (LiFePO4) designs with pure sine wave inverters. That narrows the field deliberately: older lithium-ion packs and modified-sine inverters are still sold, but they wear out faster and cause problems with some of the devices people most want to run. The table is built from each maker's published specifications.
          </p>

