        <div id="pure-sine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Pure Sine Wave and Why Cheap Inverters Fail
          </h2>

          <p>
            The power from a wall outlet is a smooth sine wave. A pure sine inverter recreates that shape closely. A modified sine inverter, still common in cheap no-name packs and some car inverters, approximates it with a stepped square wave. For a phone charger or an incandescent bulb, the difference rarely matters. For several of the loads people buy these packs to run, it matters a lot.
          </p>

          <p>
            CPAP machines, laptop power supplies with active power factor correction, variable-speed fans, some battery chargers, audio gear and anything with a small AC motor can misbehave on modified sine. The symptoms range from a buzzing hum and extra heat to a device that refuses to start, chargers that run hot or never finish, and, in the worst case, a shortened life for the device. CPAP makers in particular generally recommend pure sine power for battery backup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every pick in this guide uses a pure sine inverter, and that is the single most important filter if you are shopping beyond this list. Look for the phrase pure sine wave in the specification, not sine wave output or clean power, and be suspicious of any AC power bank that does not say which waveform it produces. Our <a href="/best-power-inverter/">power inverter guide</a> goes deeper on the waveform question for vehicle setups.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If the listing does not say pure sine wave, assume it is not.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Makers of pure sine packs advertise it because it costs more to build. A battery pack that hides its waveform is almost always modified sine, and modified sine is the wrong choice for CPAPs, laptop bricks and motors.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Pure sine also helps with efficiency on some loads, because the device's own power supply is not fighting a distorted input. That does not make the inverter lossless. Converting battery DC to 120-volt AC typically costs somewhere around 10 to 15 percent of the energy in a good small inverter, which is one reason to use DC outputs whenever you have the choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surge" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Surge, Boost Modes and Motor Loads
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two very different features hide behind the big second number on a spec sheet. One is surge capacity: the inverter's ability to deliver more than its continuous rating for a fraction of a second while a motor or compressor spins up. The other is a reduced-voltage boost mode, which EcoFlow calls X-Boost and Bluetti calls Power Lifting, that lets a high-wattage resistive load run by feeding it lower voltage than normal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Surge is the one that matters for motors. A small fridge, a fan, a pump or a CPAP blower draws a short spike well above its running wattage when it starts. A 300-watt inverter with a 600-watt surge rating can often start a device that runs at 80 watts but spikes to 400 for an instant. Whether it actually does depends on the specific motor, and manufacturers do not publish starting current for most small appliances, so treat compressor loads as something to try at home before you depend on them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Boost modes are a different trick. A 1,000-watt hair dryer fed reduced voltage will draw less power and the inverter will stay inside its limit, but the dryer runs cooler and weaker. A kettle takes longer to boil. That is useful for resistive heat in a pinch and useless for anything with electronics or a motor, which may not run properly at reduced voltage. The makers say as much in their own documentation: boost modes are for resistive loads only.
          </p>

          <p>
            So when you compare the EcoFlow River 3 at 300 watts with X-Boost to 600, and the Bluetti EB3A at 600 watts with Power Lifting to 1,200, the meaningful comparison is the continuous figure: 300 versus 600. The EB3A and the River 3 Plus are the two picks in this list that can genuinely run a 400 to 500-watt load at full voltage.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical test before you need it: plug the device you care about into the pack at home, start it several times, and watch the display. If the output figure sits comfortably below the inverter rating after startup and it never trips, you have your answer. If it trips on start, a bigger inverter is the only fix; a bigger battery will not help.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="idle-draw" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Inverter Idle Draw: The Hidden Tax
          </h2>

          <p>
            An inverter uses power just by being on, even with nothing plugged in. In small portable stations, this no-load draw is commonly somewhere in the range of several watts to around 10 watts or more, depending on the model and settings. Manufacturers rarely publish it, and it is the main reason a pack never quite delivers the runtime its watt-hour figure implies at light loads.
          </p>

          <p>
            At heavy loads the idle draw barely registers. At light loads it dominates. Run a 15-watt router from a 288-watt-hour pack and the inverter overhead can easily be a large fraction of the total draw, cutting runtime by a third or more compared with the simple watt-hours divided by watts calculation. That is the situation where USB-C or the 12-volt socket wins, because a router or modem can often run from DC through an inexpensive adapter cable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most packs also turn the AC output off automatically after a period with little or no load, to stop exactly this waste. That is good for battery life and bad for a CPAP at three in the morning if the machine drops to very low draw between breaths, or for a router that sits at a few watts. Check the app or settings for the AC auto-off timer, and turn it off when you need a low-draw device to keep running overnight.
          </p>

          <p>
            The habit that saves the most energy is simple: switch the AC output off whenever you are not using it. The button is usually separate from the USB and DC buttons for exactly this reason. A pack left with AC on in a drawer can empty itself in a day or two without powering anything at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Runtime Math in Watt-Hours
          </h2>

          <p>
            The starting formula is capacity in watt-hours divided by load in watts. A 288-watt-hour pack running a 60-watt load lasts about 4.8 hours in theory. In practice, subtract inverter losses and idle draw, and leave the bottom of the battery alone for a cold start or a final phone charge. A realistic rule of thumb for AC loads is to use 80 to 85 percent of rated capacity before dividing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Worked through for the common loads in this class: a CPAP without the humidifier at around 40 watts runs about five to six hours from a 288-watt-hour pack, which is usually one night but not two. A 60-watt laptop charging from empty refills a typical laptop battery three or four times. A 50-watt box fan runs a little over four hours. A 30-watt modem and router combination stays up for roughly seven or eight hours, more if you can power it from DC.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heated humidifiers change the CPAP math completely. With humidification and a heated hose, many machines draw two or three times as much, and a pack this size may not last the night. If CPAP is the priority, turn off the humidifier during outages, use the maker's DC cable where one exists, or step up to a larger station sized for the job.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rough runtimes from a 288Wh pack (AC, estimates)</p>
