          <p>
            Recharging speed matters as much as capacity in a long outage. All six picks here can recharge from the wall in roughly an hour to a couple of hours, and all accept solar input. A small folding panel from our <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">portable solar panel guide</a> can put most of a battery this size back in over a sunny day, which turns a one-night pack into a multi-day one as long as the weather cooperates.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="airline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Airline Limits: Why None of These Fly
          </h2>

          <p>
            The FAA rules for lithium batteries in passenger luggage are built around watt-hours. Spare batteries must go in carry-on bags, never checked. Packs up to 100 watt-hours are allowed without special permission. Packs between 101 and 160 watt-hours need airline approval and are limited to two per passenger. Anything above 160 watt-hours is not allowed on a passenger aircraft at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every pick in this guide is between roughly 245 and 290 watt-hours, so none of them can go on a commercial flight in either checked or carry-on luggage. That is true of practically every battery pack with a useful AC outlet, because an inverter big enough to matter needs a battery big enough to feed it. Some airlines publish stricter rules than the FAA baseline, and international carriers follow their own regulators, so always check with the airline before you pack any lithium battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need power on a trip that involves flying, the realistic answer is a USB-C power bank under 100 watt-hours, which covers phones, tablets and any laptop that charges over USB-C. Our <a href="/best-laptop-power-bank/">laptop power bank guide</a> lists packs that clear the limit. For a CPAP, check whether the manufacturer sells an FAA-compliant battery designed for that machine; several do, and they are the only sensible choice in the air.
          </p>

          <p>
            Driving is the opposite story. All of these packs travel happily in a car, and most can recharge from the vehicle's 12-volt socket on the way. That is where a battery pack with an outlet earns its keep: road trips, campsites, tailgates and job sites where the nearest wall socket is a long way away. For trips where you will be camped for several days, our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">camping power station guide</a> sizes up from here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Using a Portable Outlet Safely
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery packs produce no exhaust, so unlike a gas generator they are safe to run indoors, in a bedroom or in a tent. Give them a little airflow around the vents, keep them off soft bedding, and do not cover them while charging, because both the charger and the inverter shed heat through a small fan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never connect a battery pack to your house wiring through a wall outlet. A so-called suicide cord, with a male plug on both ends, can energize the plug's exposed pins and can send power back out onto the utility line, which can injure or kill a line worker. The only safe way to feed household circuits from a backup source is through a transfer switch or interlock installed by a licensed electrician, which is the subject of our <a href="/best-generator-transfer-switch/">generator transfer switch guide</a>. A pack this small is not meant for that job anyway; run extension cords to the devices instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a proper indoor-rated extension cord or power strip if you need more outlets, and add up the wattage of everything plugged in. The pack will shut off when you overload it, but a cheap thin cord can run warm long before that point. Keep the pack and its cords away from water, and do not daisy-chain power strips.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store the pack at partial charge in a cool, dry place if it will sit for months, and top it up every few months so it is ready when you need it. LiFePO4 holds its charge well and tolerates thousands of cycles, but no battery likes being stored hot and full. Check the maker's guidance on charging below freezing; lithium batteries of every type should not be charged when they are very cold.
          </p>
        </div>

