          <p>
            If weight and size come first, the <strong>EcoFlow River 3</strong> is the one to carry. It gives up a little capacity in exchange for a smaller box, and its fast switchover makes it a reasonable router or modem backup on the desk. If you already own a Jackery solar panel, the <strong>Jackery Explorer 300 Plus</strong> keeps you in one ecosystem; on a tighter budget, the <strong>Jackery Explorer 240 v2</strong> covers the same 300-watt loads with a little less battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need more AC watts than 300, skip straight to the <strong>Bluetti EB3A</strong> or the <strong>EcoFlow River 3 Plus</strong>. Both have 600-watt continuous inverters, which handles a small TV, a laptop and a fan at the same time, or a single load in the 400 to 500-watt range. The River 3 Plus is the better choice if you think you may want more capacity later, because EcoFlow sells an add-on battery for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if what you really want to run is a fridge for a day, a microwave, a space heater or a well pump, none of these is the right tool. Step up to a 1,000-watt-hour-plus station from our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">home backup power station guide</a>, or a generator used outdoors, well away from the house. A battery pack with an outlet is a superb small tool, and it is at its best when you ask it to do small jobs.
          </p>
