        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by watt-hours and ignoring the inverter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Capacity tells you how long; the inverter rating tells you whether the device runs at all. A 300-watt inverter will not run a 900-watt coffee maker no matter how big the battery is. Check the wattage on the appliance's label against the continuous AC rating before anything else.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a modified sine pack for a CPAP or laptop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cheap AC power banks often use modified sine inverters and do not say so. CPAPs, laptop bricks with power factor correction, variable-speed fans and small motors can hum, run hot or refuse to start. Every pick here is pure sine; if a listing does not say pure sine, assume it is not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating boost mode as real wattage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                X-Boost and Power Lifting run resistive loads at reduced voltage, so a hair dryer runs cool and a kettle boils slowly. They do nothing for motors or electronics. Compare packs on continuous AC output, and treat the boost number as an emergency convenience rather than a rating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the AC output switched on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An inverter draws power with nothing plugged in. Left on in a drawer, a small pack can drain itself in a day or two. Turn AC off whenever you are not using it, and use USB-C or the 12-volt socket for devices that can run on DC.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to plug the pack into the house</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Never use a cord with male plugs on both ends to feed a wall outlet. It can electrify exposed pins and send power back onto utility lines, endangering line workers. Run extension cords to devices instead, and have a licensed electrician install a transfer switch for any circuit-level backup.
              </p>
