            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What can a 300-watt portable outlet run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Laptop chargers, CPAP machines without heated humidifiers, box fans, modems and routers, LED TVs, LED lights, camera and drone chargers and most cordless tool battery chargers. It will not run hair dryers, coffee makers, kettles, toasters, microwaves or space heaters, which draw 600 to 1,500 watts. Motors and compressors depend on their startup surge.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a battery pack with an outlet run a mini fridge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes, briefly. A small fridge might run at 50 to 100 watts but spikes on compressor startup, and a 600-watt surge rating may or may not cover it. Even when it starts, a pack under 300 watt-hours will only run it for a few hours. For refrigeration through an outage, a station of 1,000 watt-hours or more is the realistic choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I take a battery pack with an AC outlet on a plane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not one of the picks here. FAA rules allow spare lithium batteries up to 100 watt-hours in carry-on bags, 101 to 160 watt-hours with airline approval, and nothing above 160 watt-hours on passenger aircraft. These packs are 245 to 288 watt-hours. For flights, use a USB-C power bank under 100 watt-hours and check with your airline.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to use a battery pack indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Battery packs produce no exhaust, so they are safe in a bedroom, office or tent, unlike any fuel-burning generator, which must only run outdoors well away from the house. Keep the vents clear, do not cover the pack while charging, and keep it away from water. Never connect it to house wiring through a wall outlet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my pack die faster than the watt-hours suggest?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The inverter loses energy converting DC to AC and draws power just by being on. At light loads such as a router or a CPAP, that overhead can take a large share of the battery. Budget about 80 to 85 percent of rated capacity for AC loads, and use USB-C or the 12-volt output whenever the device allows.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a power bank with an AC outlet better than a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are the same idea at different sizes. A small pack around 250 to 300 watt-hours is light enough to carry anywhere and handles one or two small AC devices. A larger station adds capacity and inverter output for fridges, longer outages and bigger loads, at several times the weight and cost. Buy the smallest size that covers your largest load.
              </p>
